  <div class="wrap">
<div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Translating transactions and foreign operations into the reporting currency</div><div class="principle">ASBE No. 19 governs HOW an ENTERPRISE INCLUDES foreign CURRENCY transactions and FOREIGN operations WITHIN its OWN financial STATEMENTS, and HOW it TRANSLATES financial STATEMENTS into a PRESENTATION currency â€” BROADLY converged with IAS 21 internationally, sharing the SAME functional-CURRENCY concept and MONETARY/non-monetary DISTINCTION.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Functional Currency</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">The currency of the primary economic environment</div><div class="principle">An ENTERPRISE'S FUNCTIONAL currency is DETERMINED by the PRIMARY economic ENVIRONMENT in WHICH it OPERATES â€” GENERALLY the currency that MAINLY influences SALES prices, LABOUR, material, and OTHER costs â€” CONSISTENT with IAS 21's OWN functional currency concept.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>Initial Recognition â€” Spot Rate at Transaction Date</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Translating a foreign currency transaction on first recognition</div><div class="principle">A FOREIGN currency TRANSACTION is INITIALLY recorded by APPLYING the SPOT exchange RATE at the DATE of the TRANSACTION (or a RATE that CLOSELY approximates IT, such as an AVERAGE rate for a PERIOD, WHERE exchange RATES do not FLUCTUATE significantly).</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Monetary Items â€” Retranslated at Each Reporting Date</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Exchange differences recognised in profit or loss</div><div class="principle">FOREIGN currency MONETARY items (CASH, receivables, PAYABLES) are RETRANSLATED at the CLOSING rate at EACH reporting DATE, WITH the RESULTING exchange DIFFERENCES recognised in PROFIT or LOSS for the PERIOD â€” CONSISTENT with IAS 21's OWN treatment internationally.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Non-Monetary Items â€” Historical Cost vs Fair Value</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">The measurement basis determines the translation treatment</div><div class="principle">NON-MONETARY items MEASURED at HISTORICAL cost are NOT retranslated â€” they REMAIN at the exchange RATE prevailing at the TRANSACTION date. NON-MONETARY items MEASURED at FAIR value are TRANSLATED using the exchange RATE at the DATE the fair VALUE was DETERMINED, WITH the resulting DIFFERENCE treated CONSISTENTLY with HOW other fair VALUE gains/losses on THAT item are treated (EITHER profit or LOSS, or OTHER comprehensive INCOME, depending on the ITEM'S own measurement CATEGORY).</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Translating Foreign Operations for Consolidation</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Assets/liabilities at closing rate, income/expenses at transaction-date or average rate</div><div class="principle">WHEN translating a FOREIGN operation'S financial STATEMENTS for CONSOLIDATION purposes, ASSETS and LIABILITIES are TRANSLATED at the CLOSING rate, WHILE INCOME and EXPENSES are TRANSLATED at the exchange RATE at the DATES of the TRANSACTIONS (or an AVERAGE rate as a PRACTICAL approximation) â€” WITH the RESULTING exchange DIFFERENCES recognised WITHIN OTHER COMPREHENSIVE INCOME, RATHER than PROFIT or loss.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Recycling on Disposal of a Foreign Operation</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Cumulative translation differences transferred to profit or loss</div><div class="principle">ON DISPOSAL of a FOREIGN operation, the CUMULATIVE amount of EXCHANGE differences PREVIOUSLY recognised WITHIN other COMPREHENSIVE income (RELATING to that OPERATION) is RECLASSIFIED to PROFIT or LOSS â€” CONSISTENT with IAS 21'S own RECYCLING requirement internationally, and (NOTABLY) CONSISTENT with the SAME "eventual FULL recycling" philosophy DISCUSSED in the COMPANION JMIS Modification No. 2 poster EARLIER in this SERIES.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>China's Managed Floating Exchange Rate Regime â€” a Contextual Note</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">A macro/regulatory backdrop, not an accounting divergence</div><div class="principle">CHINA operates a MANAGED FLOATING exchange RATE regime for the RENMINBI, WITH the PEOPLE'S Bank of CHINA setting a DAILY reference RATE and PERMITTING trading WITHIN a SPECIFIED band. THIS is a MACROECONOMIC and REGULATORY backdrop RATHER than an ACCOUNTING-standard-LEVEL divergence â€” the ACCOUNTING mechanics THEMSELVES (spot RATE at transaction DATE, closing RATE for monetary ITEMS) apply CONSISTENTLY regardless of the UNDERLYING currency REGIME.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Worked Example â€” Retranslating a Monetary Item</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Recognising an exchange gain on a foreign currency receivable</div>
<div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: A USD-denominated export receivable</div><div class="wk-body">A company recognises a USD 1,000,000 export receivable when the exchange rate is USD 1 = RMB 7.10 (carrying amount RMB 7,100,000). At the reporting date, the exchange rate has moved to USD 1 = RMB 7.20.</div><div class="wk-answer">The receivable is RETRANSLATED to USD 1,000,000 Ã— 7.20 = RMB 7,200,000, with an EXCHANGE GAIN of RMB 7,200,000 âˆ’ RMB 7,100,000 = <strong>RMB 100,000</strong> recognised in PROFIT or LOSS for the period.</div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Worked Example â€” Recycling Cumulative Translation Differences on Disposal</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Moving accumulated OCI into profit or loss upon sale</div>
<div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: Disposing of a wholly-owned overseas subsidiary</div><div class="wk-body">A company sells its wholly-owned overseas subsidiary, which had accumulated RMB 25,000,000 of foreign currency translation gains within other comprehensive income over its years of ownership.</div><div class="wk-answer">Upon DISPOSAL, the company RECLASSIFIES the FULL RMB 25,000,000 cumulative TRANSLATION gain from OTHER comprehensive INCOME into PROFIT or LOSS, RECOGNISING it as PART of the OVERALL gain or LOSS on disposal of the SUBSIDIARY.</div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">What ASBE No. 19 requires</div><div class="check-list"><span>Amount of exchange differences recognised in profit or loss during the period</span><span>Net exchange differences recognised within other comprehensive income, and reconciliation of the opening and closing balance</span></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cn span2"><div class="ctitle cn">Where judgement matters most</div>
<div class="two-col"><div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Determining an entity's functional currency, particularly for entities operating across multiple currency environments</span></div></div>
<div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Average exchange rate used as a practical approximation for translating income and expenses</span></div></div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Relationship to IAS 21</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Comparison â€” ASBE 19 vs IAS 21</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Feature</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-warning);">ASBE 19</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-info);">IAS 21</span></div>
<div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Monetary item retranslation</span><span>Closing rate, gains/losses to profit or loss</span><span>Closing rate, gains/losses to profit or loss â€” converged</span></div>
<div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Recycling on disposal of a foreign operation</span><span>Full recycling to profit or loss</span><span>Full recycling to profit or loss â€” converged</span></div>
</div>
<div class="note">A GENUINELY thoroughly CONVERGED standard, WITH the ONLY meaningful CONTEXTUAL difference being CHINA'S own managed FLOATING exchange rate REGIME â€” a MACRO backdrop, NOT an accounting-STANDARD-level divergence.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Where every foreign currency translation event lands</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Event</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
<div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Monetary item retranslation</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span> <span class="badge bp">IS</span></span><span>Exchange gain/loss recognised in profit or loss</span></div>
<div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Foreign operation translation</span><span><span class="badge bo">SCOE</span></span><span>Recognised within other comprehensive income until disposal</span></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s15"><span class="num">15</span><h2>Related Standards</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Standard</span><span>Connection</span></div>
<div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASBE No. 33</span><span>Consolidated Financial Statements â€” relies on this Standard's foreign operation translation mechanics</span></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s16"><span class="num">16</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cn span2">
<div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Functional currency</div><div class="glossary-def">The currency of the primary economic environment in which an enterprise operates, determining the basis for foreign currency transaction accounting.</div></div>
<div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Foreign currency translation reserve</div><div class="glossary-def">A component of other comprehensive income accumulating exchange differences arising from translating foreign operations, recycled to profit or loss upon disposal.</div></div>
</div>
</div>
